Specification Transactions

Description The transaction and the rule that every transaction balances.
Description Splits and transfers are not separate kinds of thing here: a split
Description is a transaction whose Destination is Split, with its lines in
Description TransactionSplit; a transfer is a pair of transactions each naming
Description the other as Destination.
Details
Details Tables in this file group rows into one transaction with a Ref column.
Details Ref is how a table says that two rows belong together, and nothing more
Details than that: a device of these tables, short enough to read. It is not
Details shown anywhere in the program and it is not what a transaction is stored
Details under. That is its Id, given once when the transaction is created and
Details never changed afterwards. See the rule on naming a transaction.

DataType TransactionRef
Description How a table in this specification says which rows belong to one
Description transaction. Not the stored identity, which is the Id.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value | IsValid | Notes                               |
| T1    | true    | Short names are used in these tables |
| 4091  | true    | The program assigns numbers         |
|       | false   | A row always belongs to one         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shape of a transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType ImportSource
Description Where an identifier came from. Two files describing one transaction
Description do not agree on what to call it, so the name of the source travels
Description with the name of the transaction.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value | Notes                                      |
| Ofx   | A FITID, from an OFX or QFX download        |
| Csv   | Whatever the bank's CSV calls a reference   |
| Qif   | From a QIF migration, where one is present |

Entity ImportId
Description An identifier a file gave to a transaction, and which file gave it.
Details At most one per source: a transaction has exactly one home account --
Details the one it was downloaded into or typed against -- so there is no
Details second side with an identifier of its own.
| Attribute | Type         | Default | Notes                                |
| Source    | ImportSource | Ofx     | Which kind of file said so           |
| Id        | Text         |         | As that file gave it, unchanged      |

Collection ImportIds
Description The identifiers one transaction has collected. More than one,
Description because a transaction imported from a CSV and later recognised in a
Description QFX has a name in each, and losing either would make that file
Description import twice.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| ImportId | 0       | 8       | One per source at most       |

Entity Transaction
Description What a transaction is: the account it lives in, the other side,
Description and the signed amount between them.
| Attribute     | Type           | Default    | Notes                        |
| Id            | TransactionId  | none       | Identity; given once, never changes. These tables never name one: it is given by whatever creates the transaction, so a table that stated one would be stating something no scenario controls |
| Ref           | TransactionRef |            | A device of these tables; not stored |
| Date          | Date           | 2024-01-01 | The date it is posted on     |
| Payee         | PayeeName      | none       | Optional                     |
| CheckNo       | CheckNumber    | none       | Optional                     |
| Memo          | Text           | none       | For the transaction as a whole |
| Tag           | Text           | none       | A Quicken class, kept on migration; see ImportQif |
| SourceAccount | AccountPath    | none       | The account this entry lives in: downloaded into, or typed in its register. An id underneath, the same as every account reference; see the identity rule in Accounts |
| Destination   | AccountPath    | none       | The other side: a category, a real account, or the literal Split when the parts are in TransactionSplit below |
| Amount        | Money          | 0.00       | Signed against SourceAccount; Destination carries the opposite effect by construction |
| Cleared       | ClearedStatus  | Uncleared  | Per entry: the transaction is one account's own side, and clearing is that account's bank reporting it |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two kinds of unknown
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule An unknown category and an unknown account are different unknowns
Description A charge whose category nobody has chosen is Uncategorized. A payment
Description whose other account nobody has worked out is Unassigned. They are
Description separate because they are different questions with different answers
Description and different ways of being resolved.
Details Uncategorized asks what the money was for. It is answered by choosing a
Details category, by a payee rule, or by leaving it: a spending report with a
Details line called Uncategorized is telling the truth about money that was
Details spent on something nobody classified, and Quicken's own reports have had
Details such a line for twenty years.
Details
Details Unassigned asks which account the money went to. It is answered by the
Details other side of the transfer turning up in a later import, or by a person
Details assigning it in the register. It is never answered by leaving it, because
Details money that left one account and arrived nowhere is not a fact about the
Details world -- it is a fact about what has been imported so far.
Details
Details That difference is why Unassigned is a real account and Uncategorized is
Details a category. Unassigned holds money in transit, its balance is the work
Details outstanding, and that balance is expected to reach zero. A book whose
Details Unassigned balance is not zero is not wrong: it is telling its owner how
Details many halves of transfers are still waiting for the other half.
Examples: TwoUnknowns
| TheUnknown     | TheQuestion                      | WhereItLives | ClearedBy                        |
| Uncategorized  | What was the money for           | A category   | A category, a payee rule, or left |
| Unassigned     | Which account did the money go to | A real account | The other import, or the register |

Attributes TwoUnknowns
| Attribute   | Type   | Default |
| TheUnknown  | Text   |         |
| TheQuestion | String |         |
| WhereItLives | Text  |         |
| ClearedBy   | String |         |

Attributes AccountAssignment
Description Assigning the other account of a transfer by hand, in the register.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Money from outside the book
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule Whether the other side is an account or a category
Description The other side of a downloaded row is an account when the money came
Description from or went to something this book keeps, and a category when it did
Description not. That single question decides which of the two unknowns is used,
Description and nothing else about the row decides it.
Details A payment from the bank to a credit card the book keeps is a transfer and
Details nothing else: both halves are accounts here, the money never left the
Details book, and the two halves add to nothing. The book balances because it
Details should -- that is the whole of double entry and there is no income or
Details expense anywhere in it.
Details
Details A paycheck is not a transfer. The money came from an employer this book
Details does not keep, so there is no second account for it to have come out of,
Details and the other side is a category: Income:Salary. The same goes for a
Details royalty, and for a distribution from an IRA or a trust that the book does
Details not itself hold -- money arriving from outside is income, however
Details institutional the sender.
Details
Details This is why a payee that matches no account's PaymentPayee is not a
Details failed transfer. It is an ordinary deposit, and recording it as a
Details category is correct rather than second best. Unassigned is only for a row
Details that is known to have another side in this book and does not yet know
Details which.
Examples: WhereTheOtherSideLives
| TheRow                                | TheOtherSide          | Because                               |
| A payment to a card the book keeps    | The card account      | Both halves are in the book           |
| A transfer between two bank accounts  | The other bank account | Both halves are in the book          |
| A paycheck                            | An income category    | The employer is not in the book       |
| A royalty                             | An income category    | The publisher is not in the book      |
| A distribution from an IRA not held here | An income category | The IRA is not in the book            |
| A distribution from an IRA held here  | The IRA account       | It is in the book, so it is a transfer |
| A card charge                         | An expense category   | The shop is not in the book           |

Attributes WhereTheOtherSideLives
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| TheRow       | String |         |
| TheOtherSide | String |         |
| Because      | String |         |

Scenario A deposit from outside the book is a category, not an unassigned transfer
Description A paycheck. Nothing in the book is the other side of it, so there is
Description nothing for Unassigned to be waiting for and the row is categorised
Description like any other.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                 | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking      | Checking | false       |
| Income:Uncategorized | Income | false       |
| Unassigned           | OtherAsset | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee        | Destination          | Amount  | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-15 | ACME PAYROLL | Income:Uncategorized | 3000.00 | Cleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Nothing is waiting. Choosing Income:Salary later is a category being chosen,
# not a transfer being completed, and the book balanced all along.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matching an entry in another register by hand
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule Assigning an account asks whether the other entry already exists
Description Assigning a real account to an Unassigned side says where the money
Description went. It does not say whether that account already has a row for it.
Description Both are possible and they are different outcomes, so the program
Description asks rather than choosing.
Details Making a new entry is right when the other account has no row for this
Details money -- usually because it has not been imported yet. The transfer
Details is one transaction naming the other account, shown in both registers,
Details and the second account will recognise its own row when its file is read.
Details
Details Matching an existing entry is right when the other account already has
Details the row, entered by hand or imported too long ago or too far out for the
Details windows to have paired them. Then there are two transactions describing
Details one movement of money, and leaving both would double it. They become one.
Details
Details So the register offers the other account's entries to scroll through,
Details nearest in date and amount first, and the user picks the one that is the
Details same movement of money. Picking nothing and making a new entry instead is
Details always available, because a user who cannot find it is better served by a
Details new row than by being made to choose wrongly.
Examples: AssignmentOutcome
| TheOtherAccount                | WhatHappens                                        |
| Has no row for this money      | One transaction names it, and shows in both registers    |
| Has the row, found by the user | Each points at the other's account; neither is deleted |
| Has the row, not found         | A new entry is made; the duplicate is left to be seen later |

Attributes AssignmentOutcome
| Attribute       | Type   | Default |
| TheOtherAccount | String |         |
| WhatHappens     | String |         |

Attributes EntryMatch
Description Matching an Unassigned side against an entry the user picked from
Description another account's register.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| OtherRef  | TransactionRef |         |

Scenario The entries offered to match are the other account's, nearest first
Description Scrolling someone through four hundred rows is not offering a choice.
Description The entries offered are the ones in the account being assigned, with
Description the closest in amount and date at the top.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       |
| Expenses:Uncategorized | Expense    | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Date       | Payee            | Destination            | Amount   | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned             | -1200.00 | Cleared |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Unassigned             | 1200.00  | Cleared |
| T3  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | Expenses:Uncategorized | -45.00   | Cleared |
When entries offered for : AccountAssignment Vertical
| Ref     | T1                     |
| Account | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then entries offered are : OfferedEntry
| Ref | Date       | Payee            | Amount  |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | 1200.00 |
| T3  | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | -45.00  |
# Both are in the card, so both can be chosen; the one whose amount is equal and
# opposite to the unassigned side is first, because it is the one meant nine
# times in ten.

Attributes OfferedEntry
| Attribute | Type           | Default    |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |            |
| Date      | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName      | none       |
| Amount    | Money          | 0.00       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming a transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

$WIP
BusinessRule A transaction is named when it is created and keeps that name
Description The name is the moment it was created: the date and the time to the
Description millisecond. The generator waits if the clock has not moved since the
Description last name it gave out, so no two can be the same, and a name sorts
Description into the order things were entered.
Details The name is given once, by whatever creates the transaction, and never
Details changes. That is the whole of the rule, and the reason for it is that
Details the alternatives cannot promise it. A name taken from where a
Details transaction came from -- a FITID, a CSV row -- is a fact about a file,
Details and a transaction can collect one of those from every kind of file it is
Details ever recognised in. A transaction imported from a CSV and later matched
Details in a QFX has a name in each, which is why ImportIds is a collection.
Details Identity cannot be one of several, and it cannot change when a later
Details import learns something, so it is kept apart from all of them.
Details
Details The import identifiers are still kept and still do the work they always
Details did: they are what makes the same file read twice import nothing the
Details second time. They are facts about the transaction rather than its name.
Details
Details The cost is a millisecond for each transaction created in a run. That is
Details nothing while a person types, and about forty-three seconds for the
Details forty-three thousand transactions of the business QIF -- paid once, on
Details the slowest thing the program already does.
Details
Details What it buys is worth more than the second. A transaction can be traced:
Details a name carrying the minute it was entered answers "when did this appear,
Details and what else appeared with it" without anything having had to record it.
Examples: TransactionNaming
| CreatedBy       | TheNameComesFrom              | Notes                              |
| Typing it in    | The clock, to the millisecond | Nothing else is consulted          |
| An OFX import   | The clock, to the millisecond | The FITID is kept, but as a fact   |
| A CSV import    | The clock, to the millisecond | The row id is kept, but as a fact  |
| A QIF migration | The clock, to the millisecond | A millisecond apart, in file order |

Attributes TransactionNaming
| Attribute        | Type   | Default |
| CreatedBy        | String |         |
| TheNameComesFrom | String |         |
| Notes            | String | none    |

Scenario Two transactions created in the same millisecond cannot happen
Description The generator waits for the clock rather than giving out a name it
Description has given before. So transactions created as fast as the program can
Description create them are still a millisecond apart, and in order.
When transactions are created in a row : TransactionsCreated Vertical
| HowMany | 3 |
Then the names are all different
Then the names increase
# Which is why the database can hold the Id unique and never refuse a write
# that a person did nothing wrong to cause.

Attributes TransactionsCreated
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| HowMany   | Integer | 2       |

DataType TransactionId
Description The name a transaction is stored under: the date and the time it was
Description created, to the millisecond. Sorts into creation order as text.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value              | IsValid | Notes                                     |
| 20261005T194233123 | true    | The fifth of October, to the millisecond  |
| 20240102T000000000 | true    | Midnight is a time like any other         |
|                    | false   | A transaction always has one              |
| T1                 | false   | That is a Ref, which is a different thing |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The balancing rule
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Every transaction balances
Description A transaction names one signed amount against one source account, and
Description its destination carries the opposite effect by construction. There is
Description no second figure to disagree with the first, so an ordinary
Description transaction cannot be out of balance -- it is the shape that makes
Description double entry hold, not a check run over a list of transactions.
Details What can still fail to add up is a split, because a split is the one
Details place with more than one figure that is supposed to agree with
Details another: its lines against its transaction's amount. That check, and
Details the refusal when it fails, are the split rule and the shortfall
Details scenario further down this file.
Details
Details The old shape made the same guarantee by summing the amounts of the
Details postings of one transaction to zero. Two postings that summed to five
Details dollars, or a lone transaction that summed to itself, were the two ways
Details that could fail; neither is expressible now. A transaction has one
Details amount, and the entry in the other account -- when there is one, as
Details for a transfer -- is a transaction in its own right with its own.
Examples: BalanceByConstruction
| Amount   | DestinationEffect | WhatCanDisagree | Notes                                          |
| -45.00   | 45.00             | Nothing         | An ordinary payment                            |
| 1000.00  | -1000.00          | Nothing         | A deposit                                      |
| -1200.00 | 1200.00           | Nothing         | A transfer; the other side is its own transaction |
| 0.00     | 0.00              | Nothing         | A zero transaction balances                    |
| -184.50  | 184.50            | The split lines | Only a split has a second figure to disagree   |

Attributes BalanceByConstruction
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| Amount            | Money  | 0.00    |
| DestinationEffect | Money  | 0.00    |
| WhatCanDisagree   | Text   | none    |
| Notes             | String | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entering a transaction the way the register does
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes RegisterEntry
Description What is typed on one line of a register. The register is always
Description opened on one account, which is the Account column here.
Details Payment and Deposit are the two amount columns of the register, and
Details only one of them is filled in. See TransactionRegister.
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Date      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName   | none       |
| CheckNo   | CheckNumber | none       |
| Category  | AccountPath | none       |
| Payment   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Deposit   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Memo      | Text        | none       |

BusinessRule A register entry becomes one transaction
Description How the Payment and Deposit columns turn into one signed amount.
Details The amount on the register account is the deposit less the payment.
Details The register is always opened on one account, so that account is the
Details transaction's SourceAccount and the Category column is its
Details Destination. The destination carries the opposite effect by
Details construction; it is not a second figure for the register to fill in.
Examples: RegisterEntryToTransaction
| Payment | Deposit | Amount  | Notes            |
| 45.00   | 0.00    | -45.00  | Money out        |
| 0.00    | 1000.00 | 1000.00 | Money in         |
| 0.00    | 0.00    | 0.00    | An empty line    |
| 45.00   | 10.00   | -35.00  | Both filled: net |

Attributes RegisterEntryToTransaction
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Payment   | Money  | 0.00    |
| Deposit   | Money  | 0.00    |
| Amount    | Money  | 0.00    |
| Notes     | String | none    |

Scenario A payment entered in a register
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking    |
| Date     | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee    | Harris Teeter      |
| Category | Expenses:Groceries |
| Payment  | 45.00              |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee         | Harris Teeter      |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
| Cleared       | Uncleared          |

Scenario A deposit entered in a register
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-01-31      |
| Payee    | Acme Payroll    |
| Category | Income:Salary   |
| Deposit  | 3000.00         |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-01-31      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Income:Salary   |
| Amount        | 3000.00         |
| Cleared       | Uncleared       |

Scenario A check records its number
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking     | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking     |
| Date     | 2024-02-01          |
| Payee    | County Tax Office   |
| CheckNo  | 2041                |
| Category | Expenses:Home:Rates |
| Payment  | 1250.00             |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1                  |
| Date          | 2024-02-01          |
| Payee         | County Tax Office   |
| CheckNo       | 2041                |
| Memo          | none                |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking     |
| Destination   | Expenses:Home:Rates |
| Amount        | -1250.00            |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transfers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm Transfer : Transaction
Description A transaction whose SourceAccount and Destination are both real
Description accounts. It is not a separate kind of record: it is recognised by
Description its shape, and that is why it needs no special handling anywhere
Description else.

Scenario A transfer between two accounts
Description The category column of the register holds the other account. Two
Description transactions are created, one for each account, each naming the
Description other. A transaction is one account's own entry, so a movement
Description between two accounts is an entry in each -- which is also what a
Description download of the same movement from both banks would leave, and why
Description each side can clear on its own statement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-03-01      |
| Category | Assets:Savings  |
| Payment  | 500.00          |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Date       | SourceAccount   | Destination     | Amount  | Cleared   |
| 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | Assets:Savings  | -500.00 | Uncleared |
| 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | Assets:Checking | 500.00  | Uncleared |
Then transaction is a transfer : TransferCheck Vertical
| IsTransfer | true           |
| FromAccount | Assets:Checking |
| ToAccount  | Assets:Savings |

Attributes TransferCheck
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| IsTransfer  | Boolean     | false   |
| FromAccount | AccountPath | none    |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath | none    |

Scenario A payment to a category is not a transfer
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking    |
| Date     | 2024-03-01         |
| Category | Expenses:Groceries |
| Payment  | 45.00              |
Then transaction is a transfer : TransferCheck Vertical
| IsTransfer | false |

Scenario A credit card payment is a transfer, not an expense
Description Paying the card moves money between two real accounts. Recording it
Description as an expense would count the spending twice: once when each charge
Description was categorised, and again when the card was paid.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking  |
| Date     | 2024-03-05       |
| Payee    | Visa             |
| Category | Liabilities:Visa |
| Payment  | 300.00           |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Date       | Payee | SourceAccount    | Destination      | Amount  | Cleared   |
| 2024-03-05 | Visa  | Assets:Checking  | Liabilities:Visa | -300.00 | Uncleared |
| 2024-03-05 | Visa  | Liabilities:Visa | Assets:Checking  | 300.00  | Uncleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account          | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes                |
| Assets:Checking  | -300.00    | -300.00        | Paid out             |
| Liabilities:Visa | 300.00     | -300.00        | Owed by 300.00 less  |

Scenario A transaction may not name the same account on both sides
Description An account transferring to itself would change nothing and clear
Description nothing; it is always a mistake.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1              |
| Date          | 2024-03-01      |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Assets:Checking |
| Amount        | -500.00         |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| A transfer must name two different accounts             |

Scenario A transaction to a placeholder account is refused
Description A placeholder exists to group its children in a report. Entering
Description against it makes its own balance differ from the total of its
Description children, which is the one thing a tree of accounts must not do.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-03-01      |
| Category | Expenses:Auto   |
| Payment  | 45.00           |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                             |
| Expenses:Auto is a placeholder; choose one below it |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes SplitLine
Description One category line of a split, as it is typed into the split window.
Details The amount is entered the way the register entry is: positive for money
Details leaving the account being reconciled.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |
| Memo      | Text        | none    |

Scenario A split across three categories
Description One trip to the hardware store, charged three ways. The transaction
Description carries the total against the register's account; each category is a
Description line of its split.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking            | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance  | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs      | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee   | Home Depot      |
| Payment | 184.50          |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category                  | Amount | Memo          |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 | Paint         |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  | Compost       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00  | Wiper blades  |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee         | Home Depot      |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | -184.50         |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination               | Amount | Memo         |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 | Paint        |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  | Compost      |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00  | Wiper blades |

Scenario Split lines that do not add up to the total are refused
Description The split window shows the shortfall as it is typed; this is what
Description happens if the entry is committed anyway.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking            | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance  | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs      | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee   | Home Depot      |
| Payment | 184.50          |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category                  | Amount |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                     |
| The split is 30.00 short of 184.50         |

Scenario A split with an income line and an expense line
Description A paycheque is the usual case: gross pay in, tax and insurance out,
Description and the net is what reaches the account.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary          | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Insurance     | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee   | Acme Payroll    |
| Deposit | 3550.00         |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category               | Amount   | Memo       |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay  |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld   |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium    |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 3550.00         |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination            | Amount   | Memo      |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium   |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account                | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Assets:Checking        | 3550.00    | 3550.00        | Net pay          |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00   | 5000.00        | Gross, as earned |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00    | 1200.00        |                  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00     | 250.00         |                  |

Scenario A split line may transfer to a real account
Description Part of the paycheque going straight to savings is a split line
Description naming an account rather than a category. Nothing special is needed:
Description a split line does not care which kind of account it names.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee   | Acme Payroll    |
| Deposit | 2800.00         |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category       | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 2800.00         |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination    | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A transaction can name both accounts directly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description SourceAccount, Destination and Amount are how a transaction is
Description stored: the account it lives in, the other side, and the signed
Description amount between them. A register line can therefore be read from the
Description transaction alone, with no second table to join for the ordinary
Description case.
Details A split keeps a table of its own, TransactionSplit, for its lines. An
Details ordinary transaction -- the common case -- needs no second table at
Details all: SourceAccount and Destination are both already on it.
Details
Details Every reference below -- SourceAccount, Destination, and a split
Details line's own Destination -- is an id underneath, the same as every
Details account or category reference elsewhere; AccountPath is this
Details specification's reading of it, not what is stored. See the identity
Details rule in Accounts.

Scenario An ordinary transaction names both accounts directly
Description The register's category column becomes Destination; nothing is
Description created that has to sum to anything, because there is only one row.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Date          | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee         | Harris Teeter      |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
Then no split lines exist for it

Scenario A split names Split as its destination, and its categories live apart
Description The transaction itself says Split and carries the total; the parts
Description are rows of their own, found by the transaction's id, rather than
Description folded into the transaction the way Postings folds them today.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary          | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Insurance     | Expense | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1               |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 3550.00         |
When split lines entered : TransactionSplit
| Destination            | Amount   | Memo      |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium   |
Then the split lines total the transaction's amount

Scenario A split line that is itself a transfer is matched by hand, not automatically
Description Part of a paycheque goes straight to savings. The savings account
Description gets its own, independently downloaded entry later; nothing here
Description reaches across to find it, because matching a whole account's
Description entry is already a careful, user-driven thing, and a split's line
Description is a smaller, less certain thing to be matching blind. See the
Description matching rule above.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income  | false       |
When transaction entered : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T1               |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Date          | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee         | Acme Payroll    |
| Destination   | Split           |
| Amount        | 2800.00         |
When split lines entered : TransactionSplit
| Destination    | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |
When another entry is downloaded : Transaction Vertical
| Ref           | T2             |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Savings |
| Date          | 2024-04-16     |
| Destination   | none           |
| Amount        | 200.00         |
Then entries in that account are : TransactionCount Vertical
| Account | Assets:Savings |
| Count   | 1              |
# Just the one, the download: a split line naming a real account is a line of
# Assets:Checking's split, not a row of its own in Assets:Savings' register.
# Nothing here joined them, which is the point.

Attributes TransactionCount
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Account   | AccountPath |         |
| Count     | Integer     | 0       |

Scenario Assigning the other account updates Destination
Description The last resort, and the one that always works, done the new way:
Description there is one row for this entry, and assigning the other account is
Description changing what its Destination says, not creating or editing a
Description second transaction to Unassigned.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee     | Destination | Amount   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned  | -1200.00 |
When the other account is assigned : AccountAssignment Vertical
| Ref     | T1                     |
| Account | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref         | T1                     |
| Destination | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Nothing was added and nothing was deleted: the transaction that said
# Unassigned now says which account, which is the whole of the change. The
# suspense balance goes to zero because that transaction no longer names it.

Scenario Matching an entry in another register updates both transactions' Destination
Description The card was imported weeks ago and the payment never paired. Both
Description sides already exist as their own row, each with a real account as
Description SourceAccount and Unassigned as Destination. Matching them points
Description each one at the other's account; neither row is deleted and nothing
Description is merged into one.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Checking   | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | OtherAsset | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Date       | Payee            | Destination | Amount   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned  | -1200.00 |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Unassigned  | 1200.00  |
When the other entry is matched : EntryMatch Vertical
| Ref      | T1 |
| OtherRef | T2 |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount          | Destination            |
| T1  | Assets:Checking        | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
| T2  | Liabilities:Chase Visa | Assets:Checking         |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Each keeps its own date and its own payee -- the bank's word for it on one
# side, the card's on the other. The fifteen-day gap was the reason nothing
# paired them automatically and is no reason not to point them at each other
# now. Neither row names Unassigned any more, so the suspense balance is zero.

Entity TransactionSplit
Description One category line of a split, kept apart from the transaction that
Description carries the total and the account it was entered or downloaded
Description against. Only a split has rows here: an ordinary transaction's
Description destination is a plain account or category, named directly on the
Description transaction, and needs none of this.
| Attribute     | Type          | Default | Notes                            |
| TransactionId | TransactionId | none    | Which split this line belongs to; a unique id, not a name |
| Destination   | AccountPath   |         | A category, or an account for a line entered by hand as a transfer |
| Amount        | Money         | 0.00    | Positive is money leaving SourceAccount |
| Memo          | Text          | none    |                                   |

Collection TransactionSplits
Description The lines of every split in the book.
| DataType         | Minimum | Maximum | Notes |
| TransactionSplit | 0       | 50      |       |

BusinessRule A split's lines are what is totalled against its transaction's amount
Description The transaction itself is never out of balance under this shape --
Description it names one amount against one source account, and nothing sums
Description against it. Only a split can be short or over, because only a
Description split has more than one figure that is supposed to agree with
Description another. See the existing shortfall scenario above, which already
Description checks exactly this.
Examples: SplitTotalCheck
| TransactionAmount | LineTotal | Short | Notes                    |
| 184.50             | 184.50    | 0.00  | Agrees                   |
| 184.50             | 154.50    | 30.00 | Short by thirty          |
| 3550.00            | 3550.00   | 0.00  | A paycheque split agrees |

Attributes SplitTotalCheck
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| TransactionAmount | Money  | 0.00    |
| LineTotal         | Money  | 0.00    |
| Short             | Money  | 0.00    |
| Notes             | String | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When the other side is not known
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario A transaction with no category posts to Uncategorized
Description An import knows one side of a transaction and often not the other.
Description Rather than refuse it, or leave the book out of balance, the other
Description side goes to an Uncategorized account chosen by the sign, so that
Description reports stay readable and the work left to do is visible in one
Description place. See ImportOfx.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-05-01      |
| Payee   | Unknown Shop    |
| Payment | 23.75           |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-05-01             |
| Payee         | Unknown Shop           |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking        |
| Destination   | Expenses:Uncategorized |
| Amount        | -23.75                 |

Scenario An uncategorised deposit posts to income
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-05-02      |
| Payee   | Unknown Source  |
| Deposit | 500.00          |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Date          | 2024-05-02           |
| Payee         | Unknown Source       |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking      |
| Destination   | Income:Uncategorized |
| Amount        | 500.00               |

BusinessRule Which Uncategorized account the other side takes
Description Chosen by the sign of the transaction's own amount, so an expense
Description never shows as negative income.
Examples: UncategorizedChoice
| KnownAmount | OtherAccount           | Notes                    |
| -23.75      | Expenses:Uncategorized | Money left the account   |
| 500.00      | Income:Uncategorized   | Money arrived            |
| 0.00        | Expenses:Uncategorized | A zero amount; by convention |

Attributes UncategorizedChoice
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| KnownAmount  | Money       | 0.00    |
| OtherAccount | AccountPath |         |
| Notes        | String      | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing and removing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Changing the amount of an ordinary transaction moves both sides
Description An ordinary transaction carries one amount and its destination takes
Description the opposite by construction, so editing the amount in the register
Description moves both sides at once without asking anything.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1    |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 52.10 |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -52.10             |

Attributes AmountChange
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |
| Payment   | Money          | 0.00    |
| Deposit   | Money          | 0.00    |

Scenario Changing the amount of a split does not guess which line to adjust
Description A split has more than one line, so there is no one other side to
Description move, and the shortfall is reported and the split window is opened
Description instead.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination | Amount  |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-04-02 | Split       | -154.50 |
Given split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination               | Amount |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 200.00          |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| T1 is a split; the split is 45.50 short of 200.00       |

Scenario Changing the category changes the destination
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Dining    | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 |
When category changed : CategoryChange Vertical
| Ref         | T1                 |
| FromAccount | Expenses:Groceries |
| ToAccount   | Expenses:Dining    |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1              |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking |
| Destination   | Expenses:Dining |
| Amount        | -45.00          |

Attributes CategoryChange
| Attribute   | Type           | Default |
| Ref         | TransactionRef |         |
| FromAccount | AccountPath    |         |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath    |         |

Scenario Deleting a transaction removes it from both registers at once
Description A transaction is one record naming both sides, so a delete is always
Description of the whole of it, from whichever register it is asked for. There
Description is no half of it that could be left behind.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 |
When transaction deleted : TransactionSelect
| Ref |
| T1  |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 |

Scenario A reconciled transaction warns before it is changed
Description A finished reconciliation agreed with a statement. Changing a
Description transaction inside it breaks that agreement, so it is allowed but
Description not quietly.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination        | Amount | Cleared    |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 | Reconciled |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 52.10           |
Then warned that : Warning
| Message                                                             |
| This posting is reconciled; changing it will alter a reconciled balance |

Attributes Warning
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Message   | Text |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clearing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The two sides of a downloaded transfer clear separately
Description Money leaves checking on one statement and arrives in savings on
Description another, often days apart. Each account downloaded its own entry,
Description so each side is a transaction in its own right carrying its own
Description Cleared, and one can be cleared while the other is not.
Details This is why the status is on the transaction rather than anywhere
Details else: the transaction is one account's own entry, and clearing is
Details one account's bank reporting one row of its own statement.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Checking | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Destination     | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | -500.00 | Uncleared |
| T2  | Assets:Savings  | 2024-03-03 | Assets:Checking | 500.00  | Uncleared |
When transaction cleared : TransactionSelect
| Ref |
| T1  |
Then transactions are : Transaction CompareOnly
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Savings  | Uncleared |
# A transfer entered by hand is two transactions as well -- see the transfer
# scenario above -- so this is the same shape whether the two sides were
# downloaded separately or typed once, and each clears on its own statement.
